1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Section 1 names tenses, adjectives and conjunctions — three items no earlier lesson here covers.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
All three ask for a FORM the sentence has already fixed: which tense, which degree, which link.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The sentence prints the feature that decides the blank. Find the feature before the options.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Two of the items worked here are previous-year questions with published keys; every count is computed.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Tense says when. Aspect says how. Two dials, not one.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The perfect is one bit: has, have or had. The continuous is another: the -ing form.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Twelve cells, twelve different forms — for a regular verb and for an irregular one.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
So a form question is decodable: the form names both dials.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A previous-year tense item: after he returned, he ______ to visit the temple.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The past perfect puts an event before the reference point. Simple past puts it at or after.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Exactly one of the four options places the wish after the return. The published key is that one.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The other perfect: a span that reaches now. Since and for open it; in 2019 and last year close it.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A future meaning does not take will inside an if-clause or a time clause.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Three of the six printed sentences break that rule, and the break is always in the same place.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Were you a bird, you ______ in the sky: an unreal present takes would, not shall.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
One of the four options is would plus a base verb. That one is the key.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Of the two takes the comparative; of the three takes the superlative.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Three of the five printed contexts are legal, and the page decides which by counting.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Less is already comparative, so very less marks the comparison twice.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The 2012 paper asks for the incorrect sentence, and exactly one of the four has that clash.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Some adjectives do not come in degrees at all.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Nothing is more unique, most fatal or very dead: the modifier adds a comparison the word denies.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Thirty modifiers are tried: twenty-four are rejected, all on the eight absolute adjectives.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Complete and perfect are disputed in edited usage, so the papers avoid them.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Adjectives come in a fixed order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
A wrong order is a number of inversions, and a number can be counted.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Four printed orders of four adjectives: exactly one has no inversion at all.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Published descriptions of the order disagree on age against shape, so no item turns on that pair.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The -ed form names the one who feels; the -ing form names the cause.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The students were bored; the lecture was boring. The same event, two roles.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Two of the six printed sentences swap the roles, and the page finds them by rule.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Ask who feels and what causes before you choose the ending.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Seven coordinating conjunctions join equals. Nine subordinating ones attach a dependent clause.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Only a subordinating conjunction can be fronted with its clause and followed by a comma.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
One relation needs one marker: although with but marks the contrast twice.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
But sets a contrast, so the clause after it must be negative. One option of four is.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Neither nor takes the nearer subject. As well as takes the first subject and ignores the nearer one.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Proximity is right in all four correlative sentences and wrong in both additive ones.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
The nearest-noun guess that fails on the list of items is right here. The construction decides.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Five steps for all three items: name the feature, test the options, then check once.

